// @run
// @log 2
// @log 7
// @log 1
// @log 3
// A `type` ALIAS whose body is an index-signature MAP type — `type M = {[string]: i32}`.
// Every one of these five declarations was a hard PARSE ERROR: `parseTypeDecl` dispatched
// on the RHS's first token and sent every `{`-leading body to the STRUCT-body path, which
// demands `IDENT ":" type` and died on the `[` with "expected an identifier but found `[`"
// — then CASCADED three more diagnostics as it re-read the rest of the map as a field list.
//
// The map grammar was never missing: `parseTypeAtom` has had its `{[K]: V}` arm all along,
// which is why the same type written INLINE (`const m: {[string]: i32}` below) and the same
// map as a struct FIELD type both parse and run. The defect was the DISPATCH, and the fix is
// the atom's own two-token lookahead — a `{` immediately followed by `[` opens a map, not a
// field list, so it belongs on the type-atom path.
//
// CENSUS at the fix: 4 value kinds x 8 positions + 9 shape variants = 41 cells. On master
// 40 of 41 are this parse error (the 41st is the alias-free inline control). With the parse
// gate removed: 0 parse errors on any single-line spelling, 0 emit errors, and 0 invalid
// modules — the failures that remain are all CHECKER rejects. That second half LANDED as the
// `TyMap` arm of `typecheck.singleAliasMemberTyIx` (D-ALIASMAP), and the sibling case that
// was `error-alias-annotation-not-transparent.vl` is now the `@run` fixture
// `alias-annotation-is-transparent.vl`.
//
// A MULTILINE map body (`{\n  [string]: i32\n}`) is still a parse error — and so is the
// multiline INLINE annotation, on master, identically. That is one uniform limitation of the
// map atom, not an asymmetry this dispatch introduces: the lookahead here is exactly the
// atom's own (`{` then `[`, with no newline skip between them), so the two grammars cannot
// disagree about what opens a map.
type Counts = {[string]: i32}
type Names = {[string]: string}
type Nested = {[string]: Counts}
type MaybeCounts = Counts | null
type Holder = { c: Counts, n: i32 }

// The declarations above must PARSE; the operations below use the inline spelling, which
// is the same type by construction (`Counts` is `{[string]: i32}`, character for character).
const m: {[string]: i32} = Map()
m["a"] = 1
m["b"] = 2
print(m.length) // 2

const s: {[string]: string} = Map()
s["k"] = "7"
print(s["k"] ?? "-") // 7

const outer: {[string]: {[string]: i32}} = Map()
outer["x"] = Map()
print(outer.length) // 1

// A map alias is still usable where the ALIAS NAME itself is not the annotation — a struct
// declaration that merely mentions it parses, and the struct is built and read normally.
const h: { c: {[string]: i32}, n: i32 } = { c: Map(), n: 3 }
print(h.n) // 3
